<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Wareru</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1253">1253</a>–<a href="/years/1307">1307</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Wareru was the founder of the Mon kingdom of Martaban, later called Hanthawaddy, in lower <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burma">Burma</a>. A Mon of humble origin, he seized Martaban in 1287 after the fall of the Pagan Kingdom and gained recognition from King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ram-khamhaeng">Ram Khamhaeng</a> of Sukhothai. He is credited with a code of law, the Wareru Dhammathat, and was assassinated in 1307.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/wareru.jpg" alt="Wareru" width="320" height="427" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Htawmonzel · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Wareru.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Wareru was born Ma Gadu on 20 March 1253 to poor peasants in the village of Tagaw Wun, near present-day Thaton, about 100 km north of Martaban; his background was Mon, Shan or both. Around 1272 he took over his father's trade with the Siamese kingdom of Sukhothai, found work in its royal elephant stables and rose to captain of the stables by the start of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ram-khamhaeng">Ram Khamhaeng</a>'s reign. Mon and Thai chronicles say he then eloped with the king's daughter, though George Cœdès called the story a legend and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/michael-aung-thwin">Michael Aung-Thwin</a> a probable trope.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Back home Gadu made his village into a fortified town, Donwun. When the governor of Martaban, Aleimma, planned a rebellion against Pagan, Gadu offered him his sister in marriage, lured him to a wedding at Donwun and had him killed. Gadu took Martaban, conquered Kampalani and, through his minister Laik-Gi, won recognition from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ram-khamhaeng">Ram Khamhaeng</a> as a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a> king. He declared himself king of Martaban on 30 January 1287.</p><p>Around 1290 he commissioned a law book in Mon, the Wareru Dhammathat. Allied by marriage with Tarabya of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bago">Pegu</a>, he broke the siege of Pegu by a large Pagan army, in the dry season of 1295 to 1296 by one reckoning, and drove the invaders from the Irrawaddy delta; neither Pagan nor its successors tried a major invasion of the south again until 1385. Late in 1296 he beat Tarabya in single combat on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-elephant">war elephants</a> and later executed him for plotting against him. Ruling the three Mon-speaking provinces, he gained recognition from Yuan China in 1298.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In January 1307 the two sons of Tarabya, whom he had raised, stabbed him to death in revenge for their father; they were caught and executed. Having no son, he was succeeded by his brother Gada as Hkun Law. His kingdom became the only Mon-speaking polity left after the 1290s and the wealthiest of the post-Pagan states into the 16th century, helping the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mons">Mons</a> emerge as a coherent ethnicity. His code was adopted by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-toungoo-empire">First Toungoo Empire</a>, translated into Burmese, Pali and Siamese, and shaped the later law books of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ayutthaya-kingdom">Ayutthaya</a> and Restored Toungoo Burma.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Wareru” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Mon south broke away under <b>Wareru</b>, who founded the Hanthawaddy Kingdom, and Arakan went its own way.</blockquote><a href="/empire/pagan-kingdom">Pagan Kingdom · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Wareru</b>, a Mon of humble origin, declared himself king of Martaban as the Pagan Kingdom collapsed, and Pagan, in its death throes, took no action.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/january-30">30 January in history</a></li><li><blockquote>After the fall of the Pagan Kingdom, <b>Wareru</b>, a Mon adventurer said to have been a merchant, seized Martaban in 1287 and gained recognition from Sukhothai.</blockquote><a href="/state/hanthawaddy-kingdom">Hanthawaddy Kingdom</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Thaton, in what is now <a href="/countries/myanmar">Myanmar</a>. Died in Mottama, in what is now <a href="/countries/myanmar">Myanmar</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=16.93,97.37&amp;year=1288" aria-label="Thaton on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7198 1535 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Wareru"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7648 1805h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7648 1805h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=16.93,97.37&amp;year=1288">What was here in 1288? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
